I asked @RichardMCNgo, @jacobmbuckman, @kenneth0stanley, @tylercowen, @jeremyphoward and @finbarrtimbers: "How might someone adapt their career given AGI?"

@jeremyphoward
If you're a knowledge worker you should be really deeply engaging with this technology in a very practical, grounded way. Talk to lots of other people in your space, find the very best people who are working with AI in the very best way and then try and do it even better.

@RichardMCNgo
Groups of people who trust each other and can honestly collaborate will be able to leverage AI to do incredible things. Figure out how to join or create such groups.

@jacobmbuckman
Spend more time on the interesting parts of your job. I think how boring a task is is a decent proxy for how easy it is to automate.
Try as hard as you can to have your whole job be ChatGPT in and out. And see how that goes for you. Don't be caught unawares. But if you do that and you feel you'd get bad performance reviews, then, okay, you're good until it gets better. So, focus on the areas that ChatGPT doesn't do well at.
Everyone's a generalist now. That means there's more competition for generalists.

@kenneth0stanley
Now even the safe things are uncertain. If every path is risky, you might as well just follow your interests.

@tylercowen
For future jobs, you'll have to know how to work with AI models in most jobs.
You need to be more human.

@finbarrtimbers
AI makes everyone into quasi-entrepreneurs. You're using your taste and your judgement to direct these systems. Approach your job from that perspective, identify problems that people need solving.

